Walk the list once with three pointers: `prev`, `curr` and `next`. At each node, flip its `next` pointer to point backward.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

typedef struct Node {
    int data;
    struct Node *next;
} Node;

Node *reverse(Node *head) {
    Node *prev = NULL;
    Node *curr = head;

    while (curr != NULL) {
        Node *next = curr->next;  // save the rest of the list
        curr->next = prev;        // flip the link
        prev = curr;              // advance prev
        curr = next;              // advance curr
    }
    return prev;                  // prev is the new head
}
```

Example usage:

```c
Node *push(Node *head, int value) {
    Node *n = malloc(sizeof *n);
    n->data = value;
    n->next = head;
    return n;
}

int main(void) {
    Node *head = NULL;
    for (int i = 5; i >= 1; i--) head = push(head, i);  // 1->2->3->4->5

    head = reverse(head);                                // 5->4->3->2->1

    for (Node *p = head; p; p = p->next) printf("%d ", p->data);
    printf("\n");

    while (head) { Node *t = head->next; free(head); head = t; }
    return 0;
}
```

**How it works:** Before changing `curr->next`, you save it in `next`. Otherwise you'd lose the rest of the list. When the loop ends, `curr` is `NULL` and `prev` is the old tail, which is now the head.

**Complexity:** O(n) time and O(1) extra space.

**Notes:**
- The function returns the new head, so you must assign it back (`head = reverse(head);`). Alternatively, take a `Node **head` and update it in place.
- It handles an empty list and a single-node list without special cases.
- A recursive version is possible, but it uses O(n) stack and can overflow on long lists. The iterative one is usually the better choice.

